#{[17592186350720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186348536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350720 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350720 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350720 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186349871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350720 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "08077c01-ccff-5e05-a424-ef1243bb7696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350824 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350824 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350824 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186350003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350824 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "46676d10-821d-5121-88ba-a5db1e61fc4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351222 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81106" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351222 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351222 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351222 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "820db9dd-f4cd-512d-b911-c52d62f6eb3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351222 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}}=-\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}+\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{10}}=\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354757 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354757 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354757 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a1bc7b6-4de7-5eac-8417-854e3e18c590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355774 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355774 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355774 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7da5b856-80e8-5cfd-bd82-d217ab37885e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355847 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355847 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355847 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8626180b-9f54-5542-bbda-3b519ffbbcb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356085 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356085 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356085 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a00f8753-5db0-5f2a-a18d-d3faa6c8c488" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356384 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356384 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356384 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c4d3dc9e-cb4f-5ec5-920b-00a0a9195ba2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357144 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186354757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357144 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357144 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357144 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357144 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357144 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357144 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357144 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186354757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357144 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7ee1fe3e-224e-55a4-98f8-d5e86ba6333e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357737 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357737 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357737 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=32t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+16t$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+8t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357737 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91ea3a27-d7dd-5526-bebd-b64e4b8069b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357737 #edb/ref 1074 "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$ It can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ Since the external force is a linear function, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta .\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t, \\\\ y(t) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly II matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357894 #edb/ref 1080 "0.27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357894 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357894 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1103e6c4-8e5a-5d60-a8d6-1e582b31d670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358408 #edb/ref 1080 "0.31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358408 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358408 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4beccc4b-bd42-53f7-a6ef-d29833929fbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358525 #edb/ref 1080 "0.16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358525 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358525 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "58efc89e-0bb9-5250-bf2e-d9981f1e00d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359833 #edb/ref 1080 "0.13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359833 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359833 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ea0097db-0220-5610-aa9c-bf8496bfb7cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359876 #edb/ref 1080 "0.23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359876 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359876 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ee51d506-3a45-567d-86d2-6491d5d72676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360193 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186357894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360193 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360193 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360193 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360193 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360193 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360193 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360193 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186358525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360193 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "79790386-5ca3-5b7e-a326-b45ba6bd2fed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360541 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360541 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360541 #edb/ref 1071 "The time that a particular laptop battery lasts is normally distributed with a mean of $32$ hours and a standard deviation of $4$ hours. What is the probability that a full charge lasts for $36$ hours or more? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5563fda7-bd6a-55bd-869b-0a5d94077bb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360541 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the time the full charge lasts. Then we have $X∼N(32,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X\\ge 36).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 36) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{36-32}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 1) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1)=1-0.8413≈0.1587.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a full charge lasts $36$ hours or more is approximately $0.16.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361108 #edb/ref 1080 "images/c5/c5e335ffe5f2343671d1f9eca6cedc34ef34a9700837451e963cfcfe1cd153d5.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361108 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361108 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3b5a1453-83df-5991-9fd6-135cf92a1272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361122 #edb/ref 1080 "images/eb/eb621888b04d5bd9b842624c278422995db44097adde61bbc769e8df0a5a47e7.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361122 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361122 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f0f0bcd-a732-5bb7-8af2-fe05f1453089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361267 #edb/ref 1080 "images/a7/a77fcb19143b7bdee347993a1da4fb6f73c5c1460c55ecab24ffde9afe7f6cf0.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361267 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361267 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f7dcf80-41db-5be0-ac2e-e22110ddadb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361332 #edb/ref 1080 "images/0b/0b6a1907f045e737c1f136a47d941069efbfd23a389add17d1ae31cf336763b8.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361332 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361332 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6fb068f9-4864-5875-ad12-26ac33f5a915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361508 #edb/ref 1080 "images/39/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361508 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361508 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "969fafc8-0546-5321-bf5c-6fff9f7de328" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "832aff61-5879-5808-944b-77a06173542d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362298 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362298 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362298 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362298 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+y-2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362298 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f99c9bb-9f0b-5721-8c35-4c71f8216676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362298 #edb/ref 1074 "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,1).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/39/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365253 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365253 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365253 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365253 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365253 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c0d1d1a5-d9cb-5570-acc7-da72657b9a2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365253 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/d4/d4e5b78932f8ff6155ac81e12538eb31ac211c9d27d6a2b0c67542527ccd3b2a.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2>0$ | $0$ | $-2<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (4-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =4(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+18 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8x-{x}^{2}+36 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(4)=\\sqrt{-(4)^{2}+8(4)+36}=2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186365258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "95c44088-63d3-5d47-8f73-8487757bc6ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365255 #edb/ref 1080 "8\\,m/s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365255 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365255 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ec26c32f-330e-5878-8370-d35956d6bd96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365256 #edb/ref 1080 "6\\,m/s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365256 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365256 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "255624ce-60b8-5c54-9dfe-53912d1536ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365257 #edb/ref 1080 "4\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365257 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365257 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe85ed8c-5a74-5ea1-9034-77b240f6eb4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365258 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365258 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365258 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3a46d353-568f-565f-a23b-bcdc3c812f55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365259 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365259 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365259 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d7fcf9de-02cf-52a0-b525-2fc6833847e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366132 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366132 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366132 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366132 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nSuppose that $V=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V,$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366132 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "140b78de-e11f-5db2-951c-82c8f5734177" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366132 #edb/ref 1074 "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{8}{8}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{8}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{18}{9}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{3}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 6 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{3}{2},$ $c=\\frac{3}{2},$ and $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366133 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366133 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366133 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366133 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366133 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366133 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366133 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366133 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186366138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366133 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "22bd81a8-fc97-5b1d-aab5-22729b731045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366134 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366134 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366134 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "162d9cfd-034a-5c33-9d0a-906cac7f6fc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366135 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366135 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366135 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a0e94b1d-482a-5d97-a07a-fd91a8e31af6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366136 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366136 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366136 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5e6ee2b1-5486-5bb5-8122-ecb889f89cb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366137 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366137 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366137 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09cba4a0-9f24-50ca-aaaf-b5d7a53af3e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366138 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366138 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366138 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ca0db238-7843-5a87-bfad-905b7e4e7ee9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371555 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371555 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109326" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371555 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371555 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{12}+\\frac{1}{y}$ that lies between the lines $y=1$ and $y=e.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371555 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59c768e0-123a-5f86-b9a6-1b397e77b597" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371555 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=1$ to ${y}_{2}=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{3}}{12}+\\frac{1}{y})=\\frac{{y}^{4}-4}{4{y}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{8}-8{y}^{4}+16}{16{y}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{8}-8{y}^{4}+16}{16{y}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{\\frac{{y}^{8}+8{y}^{4}+16}{16{y}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\,y\\sqrt{\\frac{({y}^{4}+4)^{2}}{16{y}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y(\\frac{{y}^{4}+4}{4{y}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{y}^{4}+4}{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}({y}^{3}+\\frac{4}{y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(\\frac{{e}^{4}}{4}+4-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}({e}^{4}+15).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371556 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371556 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371556 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371556 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371556 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371556 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371556 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371556 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371556 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cfb25814-53f0-5252-8e81-2248d22b22d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371557 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{15}({e}^{4}+1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371557 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371557 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e775560f-3f4c-54be-841d-b0c3d21fd878" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371558 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4\\pi }{15}({e}^{4}-12)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371558 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371558 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "32562cc2-5b04-5833-b156-51f71e7dd7ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371559 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi {e}^{2}}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371559 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371559 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "deec031c-a589-562b-a505-50630bb393d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371560 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{8}({e}^{4}+15)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371560 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371560 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5fd98cc9-8e2e-5c55-8146-376a748259e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371561 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi {e}^{4}}{32}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371561 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371561 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4d694888-90d9-5e06-bcb4-c0145a723e0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371865 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371865 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149725" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371865 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371865 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of the double sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}3{a}_{i}(4-{b}_{j})\n$$\n\ngiven that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{a}_{i}=30,\\,{\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}{b}_{j}=45.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371865 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9cefe9ad-c8c4-50df-9dcf-62888d4bac69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371865 #edb/ref 1074 "The product rule for double summations states that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{b}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{b}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the product rule for double summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}3{a}_{i}(4-{b}_{j}) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}3{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}(4-{b}_{j}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the rules for single summations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}3{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}(4-{b}_{j}) & =(3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{a}_{i})\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}4-{\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}{b}_{j}) \\\\  & =(3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{a}_{i})\\cdot (4{\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}1-{\\sum }_{j=1}^{12}{b}_{j}) \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 30)\\cdot (4\\cdot 12-45) \\\\  & =(90)\\cdot (48-45) \\\\  & =(90)\\cdot (3) \\\\  & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371866 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371866 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371866 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371866 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9a9613f8-bb30-5421-9518-35d93e8b06bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371867 #edb/ref 1080 "290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371867 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371867 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f2b739c5-c8b2-5d66-bd15-ef2f8e39e22f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371868 #edb/ref 1080 "270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371868 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371868 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ac2df96-9c6b-5f86-8982-4c6f89455e73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371869 #edb/ref 1080 "330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371869 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371869 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b8ae4afc-a713-5930-a923-062a26b12166" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371870 #edb/ref 1080 "310" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371870 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371870 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6ae4e75b-9802-500c-992c-40e97c52da56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371871 #edb/ref 1080 "250" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371871 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371871 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9c97ebb1-e16a-55a1-ae0c-fae857b30213" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371948 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371948 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161098" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371948 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371948 #edb/ref 1071 "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,e].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371948 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1297d2b2-529f-5b54-8024-028cb62e9052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371948 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,e].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z,$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=\\frac{1}{z}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371949 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371949 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371949 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371949 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371949 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371949 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371949 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371949 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371949 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "65f76202-5575-500d-b8ff-29047c8657a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371950 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}{ln}^{2}⁡z\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{z}}\\,\\text{d}z" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371950 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371950 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dd7ba580-03b2-59e8-a90a-67e14d5b31ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371951 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{z}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371951 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371951 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b1b392cc-003a-5891-a2ae-bafd56fa7d67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371952 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{z}}\\,\\text{d}z" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371952 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371952 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b4db97b-7408-55b6-8e37-19dcb0a18d67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371953 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371953 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371953 #edb/r